WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.016
Good validation starts before parsing code is written.

2
00:00:03.016 --> 00:00:10.932
The team should define what the program expects to accept: required fields, optional fields, value types, valid ranges, units, lengths, counts,

3
00:00:10.932 --> 00:00:13.948
enum-like values, formats, and relationships between fields.

4
00:00:13.948 --> 00:00:17.717
Without that contract, validation becomes a collection of scattered guesses.

5
00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:21.487
Narrow acceptance is easier to reason about than broad acceptance.

6
00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:27.518
When possible, prefer allowlists: known commands, known modes, known field names, known units, and known formats.

7
00:00:27.518 --> 00:00:34.681
A broad rule that accepts whatever appears convenient today often becomes hard to review after the input format grows.

8
00:00:34.681 --> 00:00:35.434
Shape matters.

9
00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:39.958
A configuration entry might require a name, a timeout, and a mode.

10
00:00:39.958 --> 00:00:42.974
An external record might require exactly three fields.

11
00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:48.251
A command-line option might require a single value and no extra trailing text.

12
00:00:48.251 --> 00:00:56.544
Required fields should be present, optional fields should have defined defaults, and unexpected fields should be treated according to a clear policy.

13
00:00:56.544 --> 00:00:59.560
Type and range matter as much as syntax.

14
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:09.738
A value may parse as a number but still be invalid because it is negative, too large, in the wrong unit, outside a business rule, or inconsistent

15
00:01:09.738 --> 00:01:10.869
with another count.

16
00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:18.031
A string may match a general pattern but still be too long for a downstream storage or display rule.

17
00:01:18.031 --> 00:01:19.539
Validation should reject ambiguity.

18
00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:27.455
Wrong units, duplicate fields, inconsistent counts, empty values where emptiness is not meaningful, and extra values that the program does not

19
00:01:27.455 --> 00:01:30.094
understand should not quietly become accepted behavior.

20
00:01:30.094 --> 00:01:34.241
After validation succeeds, convert the input into a narrow internal type.

21
00:01:34.241 --> 00:01:40.649
A validated port number, bounded size, known mode, or canonical identifier communicates more than a raw string.

22
00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:45.549
That typed representation reduces repeated checks and makes later code easier to review.

23
00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:48.565
This upfront definition also helps teams discuss compatibility.

24
00:01:48.565 --> 00:01:52.712
If a field is allowed for future use, document that policy.

25
00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:59.120
If unknown fields are rejected, make that behavior consistent so callers do not rely on accidental acceptance.
